        "original_id": "fc8574c5-e897-58e6-9bf1-48587cd598b1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Hart Crane, John le Carré, T. S. Eliot\n- Works: The Bridge, The Waste Land\n- Organizations: Security Service, Secret Intelligence Service\n- Roles: poet, author, espionage novelist\n\nFacts:\n- Hart Crane wrote \"The Bridge\" as his most ambitious work and sought to make it an epic poem in the vein of T. S. Eliot's \"The Waste Land.\"\n- Hart Crane used \"The Bridge\" to express a more optimistic view of modern urban culture than the view he found in T. S. Eliot's work.\n- David John Moore Cornwell was born on 19 October 1931.\n- David John Moore Cornwell was known by the pseudonym John le Carré and was a British author of espionage novels.\n- During the 1950s and the 1960s, John le Carré worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service.\n- During the 1950s and the 1960s, John le Carré began writing novels under his pen name.",
